A||||7||Dick McHaffie||Oil Pressure Gauge Pegged!||||1||
Z||000000||Dick McHaffie||04-11-2000||03:49 PM||||I haven't driven the 85 Fiero GT for a couple of weeks (I let my Daughter use it most of the time) and this morning once I got on the Freeway and set the cruise to 67MPH, I noticed the Oil pressure gauge completely pegged.<P>When Slowed down for an offramp, it dropped down to 40, then moved back to about 60 when I got back up to speed.<P>Do I have a problem?<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||04-11-2000||04:00 PM||||Doesn't sound like you have a problem. But the Oil pressure sender could be going for readings that high. The pressure should fluctuate with engine speed. The oil pump is mechanically driven, meaning it speeds up and slows down with engine speed. Is the pressure higher than it used to be? Could be the oil was changed and a heavier weight oil was used instead.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||04-11-2000||06:01 PM||||I've replaced the oil sending unit 3X in 13 years on my '87 GT because the oil pressure guage pegs. It is a relatively simple job.  The sending units seem to crap out with relative ease on the pre 88 V-6.  They did a modification on the 88's.  I haven't replaced that one yet.||||reg||6
Z||000003||terryk||04-11-2000||09:59 PM||||The sender. And use a GM factory sender. The aftermarket senders last about 6 months for me. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 04-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||04-12-2000||01:57 AM||||There's a fairly useless vent hole in the top of the oil pressure sender that will let water, dust, and other undesirable things into it.  That's the most common reason for it to fail.  Just seal the hole with a little silicon or RTV.  Don't know if it's the same for 88's.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 04-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||04-12-2000||01:33 PM||||I think the vent hole is to equalize pressure. Worst of both worlds.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||04-12-2000||02:13 PM||||I would also check/change the oil filter. If the by-pass ball in the filter is messed up and you have a clogged filter it will do the same thing. The reason is the sending unit is before the filter not after. Just something else to check.||||reg||1
Z||000007||wxxo||04-12-2000||06:29 PM||||the battery cable will push the top of the sending unit off just ever so slightly. it may not look like it is off but it is just enought to give you screwy signals. im going through the same thing right now. its a real pisser but its not a big deal. you have to make sure that the battery cable is not in contact with the sending unit. i just loosened up the cable and twisted it up and then tightened it back down and that was the end of my problems. sorry so long but i hope that it helped.||||reg||1
